["er.eventlog.import-anchor/1",{"authority":{"logical_scope":"entity-runtime","stream_identity":"01a0d359-c9cc-77a1-9251-a0c4fa10446c","tenant":"planning"},"completeness":"available_evidence_only","evidence":[],"instance":{"entity":"story","fields":{"body":"# Story: Which side wins, declared per entity type\n\n## Outcome\n\nNobody discovers which copy of their data won by comparing the two afterwards. The answer is a word\nsomebody typed before the first write, and a write that lost is a record they can act on.\n\n## Context\n\nA composite over two stores has to answer four questions — who is authoritative, which side is read,\nwhat happens when the remote is unreachable, and what happens when the two diverge. A default for\nany of them is a policy nobody chose, applied to somebody's data. This story is the decision that\n`Policy` has no `Default` implementation.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`Policy::new(authority, read_path, when_unreachable, on_divergence)` takes all four as required\narguments and there is no `Default`; with the remote as authority, a refused remote write never\nreaches the local copy; refusing on divergence lets no write stand unreplicated; with the local\nstore as authority, a losing replica write is **recorded as a `Divergence`**, not swallowed. R-106,\nR-107.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nMerging. A divergence is recorded and surfaced; nothing here decides which of two conflicting\nvalues is right, because that is a question about the domain and this crate does not have one.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","document":{"events":[],"fields":{"$aep":{"archived":false,"metadata":{"created_at":0,"id":"ent-a1d62644e43fa134a1c577d6046d4933","locator":"ep://planning/store/story/authority-and-conflict","provenance":{"created_by":"system","updated_by":"system"},"revision":4,"type":"aep.story/v1","updated_at":0}},"body":"# Story: Which side wins, declared per entity type\n\n## Outcome\n\nNobody discovers which copy of their data won by comparing the two afterwards. The answer is a word\nsomebody typed before the first write, and a write that lost is a record they can act on.\n\n## Context\n\nA composite over two stores has to answer four questions — who is authoritative, which side is read,\nwhat happens when the remote is unreachable, and what happens when the two diverge. A default for\nany of them is a policy nobody chose, applied to somebody's data. This story is the decision that\n`Policy` has no `Default` implementation.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n`Policy::new(authority, read_path, when_unreachable, on_divergence)` takes all four as required\narguments and there is no `Default`; with the remote as authority, a refused remote write never\nreaches the local copy; refusing on divergence lets no write stand unreplicated; with the local\nstore as authority, a losing replica write is **recorded as a `Divergence`**, not swallowed. R-106,\nR-107.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\nMerging. A divergence is recorded and surfaced; nothing here decides which of two conflicting\nvalues is right, because that is a question about the domain and this crate does not have one.\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone outstanding.\n","relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:centralized-and-hybrid-storage"}],"status":"implemented","summary":"Policy is four required words with no default; a losing write is recorded as a divergence rather than swallowed.","title":"Which side wins, declared per entity type, and what a losing write becomes"},"lifecycle_state":"implemented"},"relations":[{"decomposes":"epic:centralized-and-hybrid-storage"}],"summary":"Policy is four required words with no default; a losing write is recorded as a divergence rather than swallowed.","title":"Which side wins, declared per entity type, and what a losing write becomes"},"id":"ent-a1d62644e43fa134a1c577d6046d4933","lifecycle_state":"implemented","revision":4,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["story","ent-a1d62644e43fa134a1c577d6046d4933"]}]